@@s001 Dimensions in products between matrices
@@s002 ora:row being used,dato:the vector,ok:result cell
@@s003 A has <b>2 rows and 3 columns</b>. v is a column of <b>3 numbers</b>. Before calculating anything, I check the dimensions.
@@s004 Columns of A = <b>3</b>, length of v = <b>3</b>: they match, the product exists. The result will have as many numbers as A has <b>rows</b>: 2.
@@s005 <b>Row 1</b> of A with v, pair by pair: 1·(−1) + 2·(−2) + 3·(−3) = −1 − 4 − 9 = <b>−14</b>. It becomes the first cell of the result.
@@s006 <b>Row 2</b> with v: 4·(−1) + 5·(−2) + 6·(−3) = −4 − 10 − 18 = <b>−32</b>. Second cell. The sum from before is not touched: each row has its own.
@@s007 Result <b>(−14, −32)</b>: one number per row of A. You do not add −14 and −32: they are two different cells.
@@s008 dato:the row vector,ora:column I am using,ok:result cell
@@s009 Now the vector is on the <b>left</b>: it is a <b>row</b> of 2 numbers, that is 1 × 2. M is 2 × 3.
@@s010 Same rule as for the numbers in the middle: (1 × <b>2</b>) · (<b>2</b> × 3). 2 = 2, it can be done. The result is 1 × 3: a row with as many numbers as M has <b>columns</b>.
@@s011 Now one uses r with the <b>columns</b> of M. Column 1: 1·1 + 2·0 = <b>1</b>.
@@s012 Column 2: 1·0 + 2·1 = <b>2</b>.
@@s013 Column 3: 1·2 + 2·3 = 2 + 6 = <b>8</b>.
@@s014 Result <b>[1, 2, 8]</b>: one number per column of M. Vector on the right → one uses the rows; vector on the left → one uses the columns.
@@s015 dato:first factor,due:second factor,ora:the numbers in the middle,ok:result,no:not defined
@@s016 ok:it can be done,no:not defined
@@s017 ora:the row being calculated,dato:the vector,ok:right result,no:wrong sum
@@s018 dato:shapes,ok:it works,no:error
@@s019 dato:X and y,ora:intermediate product,ok:w
@@s020 Dimensions in products between matrices
@@s021 Before multiplying, check the dimensions: five seconds are enough. It is the most common error in hand exercises, and it is also the most common <code>ValueError</code> in NumPy.
@@s022 The basic rule
@@s023 Your notes, put here
@@s024 A product <span class="m">(m × n) · (p × q)</span> is defined only if <b>n = p</b>, that is if the two numbers "in the middle" coincide. The result has the dimensions of the two <b>outer</b> numbers: <b>m × q</b>.
@@s025 (2 × 3) · (3 × 1) ✅ → result 2 × 1 · (2 × 3) · (2 × 1) ❌ → not defined.
@@s026 In practice: <b>columns of the first = rows of the second</b>.
@@s027 The check in three moves
@@s028 I write the dimensions next to each other, always in the order <b>rows × columns</b>.
@@s029 in the middle: <mark class="ora">3</mark> = <mark class="ora">3</mark> <mark class="ok">✓ the product exists</mark>
@@s030 Each cell of the result is a row of the first times a column of the second, <b>pair by pair</b>. A row of the first has n numbers, a column of the second has p: to make the pairs we need n = p.
@@s031 One cell for each pair (row of the first, column of the second): 2 rows × 1 column.
@@s032 (2 × <mark class="no">3</mark>) · (<mark class="no">2</mark> × 1): 3 ≠ 2 ⇒ <mark class="no">not defined</mark>
@@s033 A row has 3 numbers, a column has 2: they cannot be paired. Stop here and answer "not defined".
@@s034 The trick of the dimensions that cancel
@@s035 Write the dimensions under each factor and cancel the pairs in the middle: <span class="m">(m × <s>n</s>)(<s>n</s> × q) → m × q</span>. It works also with more factors: <span class="m">(2 × <s>3</s>)(<s>3</s> × <s>4</s>)(<s>4</s> × 1) → 2 × 1</span>.
@@s036 The numbers in the middle and the numbers outside
@@s037 Vector on the right: matrix · vector
@@s038 The vector is a <b>column</b>. Its length must be equal to the number of <b>columns</b> of the matrix. The result has as many elements as the matrix has <b>rows</b>: <b>one number per row</b>.
@@s039 [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]] · (−1, −2, −3)
@@s040 Vector on the left: vector · matrix
@@s041 The vector is a <b>row</b>. Its length must be equal to the number of <b>rows</b> of the matrix. It is always the rule of the numbers in the middle: <span class="m">(1 × <b>2</b>) · (<b>2</b> × 3) ✅ → 1 × 3</span>. The result has <b>one number per column</b>.
@@s042 [1, 2] · [[1, 0, 2], [0, 1, 3]]
@@s043 All the operations in a table
@@s044 Operation
@@s045 When it can be done
@@s046 Dimension of the result
@@s047 <b>identical dimensions</b> (same rows and same columns)
@@s048 the same
@@s049 <b>c · A</b> (number times matrix)
@@s050 always
@@s051 the same as A
@@s052 columns of A = rows of B
@@s053 rows of A × columns of B
@@s054 <b>A · v</b> (vector on the right)
@@s055 columns of A = length of v
@@s056 length = rows of A
@@s057 <b>v · A</b> (vector on the left)
@@s058 length of v = rows of A
@@s059 length = columns of A
@@s060 <b>u · w</b> (inner product)
@@s061 same length
@@s062 a number
@@s063 <b>Aᵀ</b> (transpose)
@@s064 always
@@s065 m × n becomes n × m
@@s066 Addition and subtraction require identical dimensions because one operates <b>cell by cell</b>: each cell of A needs its partner in B.
@@s067 Addition is not the product rule plus a constraint
@@s068 They are two <b>independent</b> rules. With <span class="m">A: n × m</span> and <span class="m">B: p × q</span>: addition asks for <b>n = p and m = q</b>; the product asks only for <b>m = p</b>. The same pair can pass one check and fail the other:
@@s069 Shapes
@@s070 ✅ identical shapes
@@s071 ❌ in the middle 3 ≠ 2
@@s072 ❌ different shapes
@@s073 ✅ in the middle 3 = 3 → 2 × 2
@@s074 <b>To remember it</b>: in addition the two shapes are put one <b>above</b> the other and must coincide, both; in the product they are put one <b>next to</b> the other and only the numbers <b>in the middle</b> must coincide.
@@s075 If a matrix is n × m, must the columns of n be equal to the rows of m?
@@s076 <span class="m">n × m</span> is the shape of <b>a single</b> matrix: n rows and m columns. The × sign is read "by", but it does not multiply two matrices. To talk about two matrices we need four numbers, two for each. The constraint "columns of the first = rows of the second" holds only for the <b>product</b>. Addition and subtraction do not "extend" it: they only ask for identical shapes.
@@s077 The four combinations were checked with NumPy: <code>A + B</code> → <code>(2, 3)</code>, <code>A @ C</code> → <code>(2, 2)</code>; <code>A @ B</code> and <code>A + C</code> → <code>ValueError</code>.
@@s078 What is NOT done
@@s079 Never add rows, columns or zeros to make the dimensions match: one would change the problem. If the numbers in the middle do not coincide, the answer is <b>"not defined"</b>.
@@s080 The same holds for the <b>transpose</b>: with A 2 × 3 and C 3 × 2, <span class="m">A + C</span> is not defined. <span class="m">A + Cᵀ</span> instead can be done (Cᵀ is 2 × 3), but it is a <b>different operation</b>, with different numbers. One transposes only if the text asks for it.
@@s081 After the T must I add from top to bottom?
@@s082 No. Addition has no direction: it pairs by <b>position</b> (same row, same column). Cᵀ, once written, is a normal matrix and is read like the others. The intuition is right only with respect to the <b>original</b> C: row i of A goes with column i of C, that is <span class="m">A<sub>ij</sub> + C<sub>ji</sub></span>. Two roads, same result: write Cᵀ and add normally (recommended), or pair the row of A with the column of C. The "direction" row → / column ↓ exists only in the <b>product</b>.
@@s083 And if I use the T on the other matrix?
@@s084 Addition becomes possible: <span class="m">A = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]</span>, <span class="m">C = [[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]]</span>, <span class="m">A + Cᵀ = [[2, 4, 6], [8, 10, 12]]</span>. But you have calculated <span class="m">A + Cᵀ</span>, not <span class="m">A + C</span>. If the question is <span class="m">A + C</span>, the answer stays "not defined". (Checked with NumPy.)
@@s085 A, B, C and v: what can be done
@@s086 The typical error
@@s087 One adds <b>inside</b> a single row × column (it is the inner product), <b>never between different rows</b>: each row produces a separate cell of the result.
@@s088 Right: (8, 14)
@@s089 row 1: 4·4 + (−4)·2 = 16 − 8 = <b>8</b><br>row 2: 4·4 + (−1)·2 = 16 − 2 = <b>14</b>
@@s090 2 × 2 matrix times vector of 2 → vector of 2.
@@s091 Wrong: 22
@@s092 8 + 14 = 22 adds the results of <b>two different rows</b>.
@@s093 A single number comes out only from the inner product of two vectors. From a matrix times a vector a <b>vector</b> always comes out.
@@s094 (8, 14), not 22
@@s095 In NumPy
@@s096 NumPy applies exactly the same rules, with two particularities. The matrix product is written <code>@</code> (never <code>*</code>, which multiplies cell by cell). And a 1D array, of shape <code>(n,)</code>, is neither row nor column: NumPy treats it as a column if it is on the right of <code>@</code> and as a row if it is on the left. When something does not work, the first check is always the <code>.shape</code> of each factor.
@@s097 The same rules, and the real errors (NumPy 2.4)
@@s098 <code>.shape</code> gives the dimensions as (rows, columns). A 1D vector has shape <code>(3,)</code>: a single dimension, neither row nor column.
@@s099 <code>@</code> is the matrix product. (2 × 3) · (3) → 2: the same −14 and −32 of the scene.
@@s100 Here v is on the left, so it acts as a row: (1 × 3) · (2 × 3). In the middle 3 ≠ 2. "size 2 is different from 3" is exactly the check of the numbers in the middle.
@@s101 Addition between different shapes: NumPy refuses. (With compatible shapes NumPy "widens" one of the two, the <i>broadcasting</i>: see the page on NumPy.)
@@s102 A 1D vector of length 2 on the left acts as a row: (1 × 2) · (2 × 3) → 3 numbers, [1·1 + 2·4, 1·2 + 2·5, 1·3 + 2·6] = [9, 12, 15].
@@s103 All the results and error messages were obtained by running the code (NumPy 2.4.6).
@@s104 The shapes in NumPy
@@s105 Where you need it in the course
@@s106 Every formula of the course with matrices and vectors can be checked with the shapes, without remembering it by heart: if the dimensions do not match in some step, the formula is written wrong. With N training points and d parameters, X is always N × d, w has d numbers and y has N. The step-by-step check of the least squares formula:
@@s107 Dimension check in w = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy
@@s108 N training points, d parameters. In the example of the <a href="w04-polinomi.html">polynomials</a> with 4 points: X is 4 × 3.
@@s109 One prediction for each point: it comes back with y.
@@s110 Xᵀ has the dimensions swapped. The result is square: that is why one can try to invert it.
@@s111 One number for each parameter.
@@s112 A vector of d numbers comes out: the weights. With the 4 points of the polynomials: 3 weights, [0.9, 0.9, 0.5] (checked with NumPy).
